<header><p class="enc-kicker">Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gosanke</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gosanke, the 'three honourable houses', were the three senior branch families of the Tokugawa, founded by sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>: the Owari, Kii and Mito houses. They ranked above all other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> and could supply an heir if the main line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shoguns</a> died out, as happened in 1716 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshimune">Tokugawa Yoshimune</a> of Kii became shogun.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gosanke.jpg" alt="Gosanke" width="198" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mon-Tokugawa.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
